The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (domain->type == IOMMU_DOMAIN_IDENTITY)
    return;
dmar_domain = to_dmar_domain(domain);

Reason:
The condition only returns early for identity domains, but the global static blocked domain (IOMMU_DOMAIN_BLOCKED) is also a dummy domain without a corresponding dmar_domain structure. When `domain_remove_dev_pasid` is called on a blocked domain, the code proceeds to call `to_dmar_domain(domain)`, which attempts to access memory beyond the `iommu_domain` structure, causing an out-of-bounds access and a general protection fault (oops).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (domain->type == IOMMU_DOMAIN_IDENTITY || domain->type == IOMMU_DOMAIN_BLOCKED)
    return;

Reason:
By extending the early return condition to include `IOMMU_DOMAIN_BLOCKED`, the function now correctly handles all dummy domains (identity and blocked) that lack associated `dmar_domain` metadata. This prevents the unsafe `to_dmar_domain()` call and avoids the out-of-bounds access and subsequent oops.

